# Upstream submission for ROCm/rocm-ernic.  NOT applied by anything in this
# repo -- this repo's build does not include rocm-ernic.  It is kept here
# because udev/99-zz-nixl-ernic-name.rules is a local workaround that exists
# only until this lands; the two should be read together, and the override
# deleted when this merges.
#
# Generated against ROCm/rocm-ernic e80aacc (default branch == develop).
# Apply with:  git apply /path/to/this.patch      (from a rocm-ernic checkout)
#
# Suggested commit message:
#
#     udev: name the RDMA device rocm_ernic<n>, not rocm-rdma-ernic<n>
#
#     The old name silently broke both RDMA transports, for two unrelated
#     reasons, and neither reported a failure -- a benchmark still ran and
#     still printed numbers, just not over this NIC.
#
#     libfabric's vrb_cmp_domain_and_dev_name() truncates a domain name at
#     its LAST hyphen before comparing it to the device name.  That is meant
#     to strip the "-xrc"/"-dgram" domain suffixes, but it cuts any hyphen,
#     so "rocm-rdma-ernic0" was compared as 9 characters against a
#     16-character device name and failed to match its own device.
#     vrb_info_add_dev_addr() was then never called, src_addr stayed NULL,
#     rdma_bind_addr() was skipped and the endpoint was exported as
#     0.0.0.0:<port>, leaving the peer to pick a source device off the
#     routing table -- in a guest, the virtio management NIC.  Affects
#     libfabric v1.22.0, v2.1.0 and main.
#
#     UCX's UCT_MD_NAME_MAX is 16 and that is the buffer size including the
#     NUL, so 15 usable characters.  "rocm-rdma-ernic0" is exactly 16, so
#     ucs_snprintf_zero() in uct/ib/base/ib_md.c stored "rocm-rdma-ernic",
#     the exact strcmp in uct_ib_get_device_by_name() never matched, and UCX
#     dropped the memory domain with a bare continue and fell back to TCP.
#     Indices 0-9 are all exactly 16 characters, so every single-NIC guest
#     hit it.
#
#     rocep1s0, ionic_0 and mlx5_0 clear both checks, which is why this only
#     appeared once the rule began handing out a long hyphenated name.
#
#     rocm_ernic<n> is 11 characters with no hyphen.  The rule's idempotency
#     guard and its free-index probe move with it -- changing only the rename
#     would make the rule re-rename on every trigger.  The constraint is
#     documented in the rules file so it is not reintroduced.
#
#     The Ethernet name rocm-ernic<n> is deliberately unchanged: netdev names
#     go through neither code path, and keeping the two textually distinct
#     makes logs readable.
#
#     Name-matching device lookups accept both spellings so guests
#     provisioned before this change keep working until their rules are
#     updated; scripts/find-rdma-device.sh, which matches on the PCI tuple,
#     remains the rename-immune way to do this and should be preferred.
#
diff --git a/.github/workflows/system-tests.yml b/.github/workflows/system-tests.yml
index 61833c0..61fb234 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/system-tests.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/system-tests.yml
@@ -743,7 +743,7 @@ jobs:
         # Identify the device by the emulated PCI tuple rather than by name.  The
         # name is rewritten twice during boot -- kernel ionic_%d, then
         # 60-rdma-persistent-naming.rules to rocep<bus>s<slot>, then
-        # 99-rocm-ernic.rules to rocm-rdma-ernic<n> -- so it says nothing
+        # 99-rocm-ernic.rules to rocm_ernic<n> -- so it says nothing
         # about which driver bound the device, and any name pattern is a
         # snapshot of one moment in that sequence.  A pattern that
         # silently stops matching is exactly how a guest with a working
diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index eb700d4..108b80e 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -111,6 +111,22 @@
 
 ### Changed
 
+* The RDMA device is now named `rocm_ernic<n>` rather than
+  `rocm-rdma-ernic<n>`. The old name silently broke both RDMA transports:
+  libfabric's `vrb_cmp_domain_and_dev_name()` truncates a domain name at its
+  last hyphen before comparing it to the device name, so the verbs domain
+  failed to match its own device, `src_addr` was left NULL and the endpoint
+  bound `0.0.0.0`; and UCX's `UCT_MD_NAME_MAX` is 16 including the NUL, so the
+  16-character name was truncated by one and the exact `strcmp` in
+  `uct_ib_get_device_by_name()` never matched, dropping the memory domain and
+  falling back to TCP without an error. Neither reported a failure. The new
+  name is hyphen-free and 11 characters; `udev/99-rocm-ernic.rules` documents
+  the constraint so it is not reintroduced. The Ethernet name
+  `rocm-ernic<n>` is unchanged -- netdev names go through neither code path.
+  Device lookups that match on name accept both spellings for now; prefer
+  `scripts/find-rdma-device.sh`, which matches on the PCI tuple and is immune
+  to renaming. Guests provisioned before this change keep the old name until
+  their udev rules are updated and the device is re-triggered.
 * The emulated device now identifies as `1dd8:1002` with subsystem
   `1dd8:5400`, rather than `1022:8001`. A guest image built for an older
   release needs its udev rules updated to match and should refresh `pci.ids`
