@@ -7,7 +7,50 @@
 # Renames network interfaces and InfiniBand devices
 # created by rocm-ernic (1dd8:1002, upstream ionic driver)
 # to stable names, so guests always see rocm-ernic<n> and
-# rocm-rdma-ernic<n>.
+# rocm_ernic<n>.
+#
+# THE RDMA NAME IS CONSTRAINED.  It must contain no
+# hyphen and be at most 15 characters.  Both come
+# from consumers, not from us, and both failures are
+# silent -- the benchmark still runs and still prints
+# numbers, just not over this NIC:
+#
+#   * libfabric prov/verbs/src/verbs_info.c,
+#     vrb_cmp_domain_and_dev_name(), truncates the
+#     domain name at the LAST hyphen before comparing
+#     it to the device name.  That is meant to strip
+#     the "-xrc"/"-dgram" domain suffixes, but it cuts
+#     any hyphen, so "rocm-rdma-ernic0" was compared
+#     as 9 characters against a 16-character device
+#     name and failed to match its OWN device.
+#     vrb_info_add_dev_addr() is then never called,
+#     src_addr stays NULL, rdma_bind_addr() is skipped,
+#     and the endpoint is exported as 0.0.0.0:<port>;
+#     the peer picks a source device off the routing
+#     table instead -- in a guest, the virtio
+#     management NIC.  Affects v1.22.0, v2.1.0 and main.
+#
+#   * UCX src/uct/api/uct_def.h sets UCT_MD_NAME_MAX to
+#     16, and that is the buffer size INCLUDING the NUL,
+#     so 15 usable characters.  "rocm-rdma-ernic0" is
+#     exactly 16, so ucs_snprintf_zero() in
+#     uct/ib/base/ib_md.c stored "rocm-rdma-ernic", the
+#     exact strcmp in uct_ib_get_device_by_name() never
+#     matched, and UCX dropped the memory domain with a
+#     bare `continue` and fell back to TCP in silence.
+#     Indices 0-9 were all exactly 16 characters, so
+#     every single-NIC guest hit it.
+#
+# "rocm_ernic<n>" is 11 characters with no hyphen.
+# rocep1s0, ionic_0 and mlx5_0 clear both checks too,
+# which is why neither bug surfaced before this rule
+# started handing out a long hyphenated name.
+#
+# The ETHERNET name keeps its hyphen on purpose: netdev
+# names go through neither code path, 15 characters is
+# the kernel IFNAMSIZ limit rather than UCX's, and
+# keeping the two textually distinct makes it obvious
+# which is which in logs.
 #
 # Install with:
 #   sudo cp udev/99-rocm-ernic.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
